From: Dianqk <dianqk@dianqk.net>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] backlight: qcom-wled: complete OVP IRQ state tracking

The downstream IRQ balance fix tracks whether delayed work needs to
re-enable the OVP IRQ. Record the initial disable when the module is
off during IRQ configuration, or the first enable leaves it masked.

Also use the tracked IRQ state after cancelling delayed work instead
of treating a successful cancellation as proof that the IRQ is already
disabled. The bootloader may leave the module and IRQ enabled before
the first brightness update schedules work. Blanking before that work
runs must still disable the IRQ.

Keep the existing delayed enable and protection thresholds. This is a
separate follow-up to the downstream IRQ balance patch.

Link: https://gitlab.com/sdm845-mainline/linux/-/commit/2712eead924a6c830ce93d0f9d794b0a23a57acc
Signed-off-by: Dianqk <dianqk@dianqk.net>
---
diff --git a/drivers/video/backlight/qcom-wled.c b/drivers/video/backlight/qcom-wled.c
--- a/drivers/video/backlight/qcom-wled.c
+++ b/drivers/video/backlight/qcom-wled.c
@@ -325,8 +325,8 @@
 			 */
 			schedule_delayed_work(&wled->ovp_work, HZ / 100);
 		} else {
-			if (!cancel_delayed_work_sync(&wled->ovp_work) &&
-			    !wled->ovp_irq_disabled) {
+			cancel_delayed_work_sync(&wled->ovp_work);
+			if (!wled->ovp_irq_disabled) {
 				disable_irq(wled->ovp_irq);
 				wled->ovp_irq_disabled = true;
 			}
@@ -1656,8 +1656,10 @@
 		return rc;
 
 	/* Keep OVP irq disabled until module is enabled */
-	if (!(val & WLED3_CTRL_REG_MOD_EN_MASK))
+	if (!(val & WLED3_CTRL_REG_MOD_EN_MASK)) {
 		disable_irq(wled->ovp_irq);
+		wled->ovp_irq_disabled = true;
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
